1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,088
Suppose a backup copy of the heeyID database leaves the bank on a disk.

2
00:00:05,138 --> 00:00:06,960
Nobody should have taken it.

3
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:12,960
On it are the customers of the bank: names, passport data, addresses and phone numbers.

4
00:00:13,010 --> 00:00:14,832
Aziz is one of them.

5
00:00:14,882 --> 00:00:18,704
What can the person with the disk read?

6
00:00:18,754 --> 00:00:26,096
Lesson 5 showed the adapters that talk to MyID, TAF, the SMS vendor and the push service.

7
00:00:26,146 --> 00:00:28,544
Each adapter takes its credential from Vault.

8
00:00:28,594 --> 00:00:33,032
Now Vault opens, and the keys inside it answer the question of the disk.

9
00:00:33,082 --> 00:00:36,968
Vault is a safe for keys, and it runs inside the bank.

10
00:00:37,018 --> 00:00:38,888
Each zone gets its own new Vault.

11
00:00:38,938 --> 00:00:42,224
The security team of the bank holds the keys that unlock it.

12
00:00:42,274 --> 00:00:45,272
The vendor that wrote heeyID never sees one of them.

13
00:00:45,322 --> 00:00:50,072
This is the line in the design that matters most: the bank keeps the keys.

14
00:00:50,122 --> 00:00:53,216
Vault holds nine kinds of keys for each zone.

15
00:00:53,266 --> 00:00:55,712
Three of them lock what the thief wants to read.

16
00:00:55,762 --> 00:01:03,224
First, the verified identity of Aziz: his names, passport data, personal identification number and address.

17
00:01:03,274 --> 00:01:07,352
His record has its own data key, and that key encrypts the record.

18
00:01:07,402 --> 00:01:10,688
The data key is stored next to the record, but locked.

19
00:01:10,738 --> 00:01:15,152
A second key locks it: the key-encryption key of the installation.

20
00:01:15,202 --> 00:01:18,104
The key-encryption key never leaves Vault.

21
00:01:18,154 --> 00:01:22,040
To read the record, the platform sends the locked data key to Vault.

22
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:27,080
Vault gives it back open for this one request, and the platform then forgets it.

23
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:34,064
This is envelope encryption: each record has its own key, and only Vault opens the envelope around that key.

24
00:01:34,114 --> 00:01:37,088
Vault also records each time it opens a data key.

25
00:01:37,138 --> 00:01:42,896
On the disk, the thief has locked records and locked data keys, and nothing that opens them.

26
00:01:42,946 --> 00:01:44,768
Second, search.

27
00:01:44,818 --> 00:01:50,552
The platform must find a customer by his personal identification number, among millions of customers.

28
00:01:50,602 --> 00:01:54,800
But that number is encrypted, so the platform cannot compare it.

29
00:01:54,850 --> 00:02:01,400
So it also keeps a blind index: a fingerprint of the number, made with a secret index key from Vault.

30
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:06,368
The same number always gives the same fingerprint, so an exact search still works.

31
00:02:06,418 --> 00:02:08,936
A fingerprint never gives back the number.

32
00:02:08,986 --> 00:02:12,296
Without the index key, nobody can make a fingerprint at all.

33
00:02:12,346 --> 00:02:14,216
Third, the one-time codes.

34
00:02:14,266 --> 00:02:20,408
The platform keeps each code only as a fingerprint, and Lesson 9 shows the rules for codes.

35
00:02:20,458 --> 00:02:25,976
A code has only a few digits, so a thief can try every possible code in a moment.

36
00:02:26,026 --> 00:02:31,736
That is why each fingerprint mixes in a pepper: a secret from Vault that is never on the disk.

37
00:02:31,786 --> 00:02:34,832
Without the pepper, all those tries give nothing.

38
00:02:34,882 --> 00:02:37,592
The other six kinds of keys do different work.

39
00:02:37,642 --> 00:02:43,496
One key seals the audit trail, so that a changed record shows, and Lesson 21 shows it.

40
00:02:43,546 --> 00:02:50,504
Keycloak signs each token with its own keys, and a secret from Vault encrypts those keys in its database.

41
00:02:50,554 --> 00:02:54,224
The credentials of the adapters from Lesson 5 live here too.

42
00:02:54,274 --> 00:02:59,192
Three more kinds work only between the units of the platform, such as their certificates.

43
00:02:59,242 --> 00:03:03,392
No key is ever in the code, in a log, or in an app on a phone.

44
00:03:03,442 --> 00:03:06,224
The passwords on the disk need no key at all.

45
00:03:06,274 --> 00:03:12,872
The platform keeps only a slow, salted fingerprint of each password, and Lesson 11 shows it.

46
00:03:12,922 --> 00:03:16,712
There is no key to steal, because there is nothing to decrypt.

47
00:03:16,762 --> 00:03:21,104
Keys also grow old, and the bank sets how often each one changes.

48
00:03:21,154 --> 00:03:28,400
When the key-encryption key changes, Vault keeps its old versions, and the platform locks the data keys again in the background.

49
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:30,272
Back to the disk.

50
00:03:30,322 --> 00:03:36,848
The thief can read the phone number of Aziz, and a few masked parts, such as the last four digits of his document.

51
00:03:36,898 --> 00:03:43,016
He cannot read the name, the passport data, the address or the full identification number of Aziz.

52
00:03:43,066 --> 00:03:46,520
Every key that could open them stays in the Vault of the bank.

53
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:50,912
For the identity of Aziz, the disk is only lost hardware.

54
00:03:50,962 --> 00:03:55,496
Suppose the thief also guesses the personal identification number of Aziz.

55
00:03:55,546 --> 00:04:00,064
Can he use that guess to find a record on the disk?

56
00:04:00,114 --> 00:04:06,352
No: without the index key from Vault, he cannot make the fingerprint, so his guess matches nothing.

57
00:04:06,402 --> 00:04:11,896
One Vault for each zone, inside the bank, and the bank holds the keys that unlock it.

58
00:04:11,946 --> 00:04:21,952
Envelope encryption locks each identity, a blind index finds a number without showing it, and a pepper makes the code fingerprints useless to a thief.

59
00:04:22,002 --> 00:04:25,264
No key sits in the code, in a log, or on a phone.

60
00:04:25,314 --> 00:04:31,240
The mobile bank holds no key, yet it calls heeyID at each step of the journey of Aziz.

61
00:04:31,290 --> 00:04:35,824
Lesson 7 shows the API contract that the mobile bank calls.
